1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,118
Dana was scrolling through the new batch on her lunch break, half-checking, half

2
00:00:05,118 --> 00:00:08,039
just looking, when the fourth static stopped her.

3
00:00:08,039 --> 00:00:12,091
The sock in it was blue, definitely blue, but it wasn't Harlan Navy.

4
00:00:12,091 --> 00:00:17,098
It was a lighter, more generic royal blue, the kind any supermarket brand could use.

5
00:00:17,098 --> 00:00:18,230
She kept scrolling.

6
00:00:18,230 --> 00:00:20,495
Six more statics had the same problem.

7
00:00:20,495 --> 00:00:22,164
The fix isn't starting over.

8
00:00:22,164 --> 00:00:25,978
It's smaller than that, and it's worth seeing exactly how small.

9
00:00:25,978 --> 00:00:30,806
Dana's first instinct was to junk the whole batch and start again, and it's worth

10
00:00:30,806 --> 00:00:35,097
naming why that instinct is wrong before walking through the actual fix.

11
00:00:35,097 --> 00:00:38,137
Thirteen statics already held the colour correctly.

12
00:00:38,137 --> 00:00:43,024
Regenerating all twenty to fix seven would throw away work that was already right,

13
00:00:43,024 --> 00:00:46,362
on top of paying for generation you didn't need to redo.

14
00:00:46,362 --> 00:00:51,249
The actual job is narrower: lock down what "correct" means precisely enough that a

15
00:00:51,249 --> 00:00:54,050
second, smaller batch can't drift the same way.

16
00:00:54,050 --> 00:00:57,626
This distinction matters beyond just saving generation time.

17
00:00:57,626 --> 00:01:02,514
Treating a partial failure as a reason to start over is a habit that scales badly.

18
00:01:02,514 --> 00:01:07,401
On a real account, running dozens of batches a month, that habit is the difference

19
00:01:07,401 --> 00:01:11,037
between an afternoon of fixes and a permanently slow process.

20
00:01:11,587 --> 00:01:16,487
Here's the actual change, and it's smaller than "colour drift" sounds like it should

21
00:01:16,487 --> 00:01:16,954
require.

22
00:01:16,954 --> 00:01:20,862
"Navy" is an instruction a model can interpret several honest ways.

23
00:01:20,862 --> 00:01:25,587
A hex code, attached to a reference image and paired with an explicit instruction

24
00:01:25,587 --> 00:01:27,279
not to reinterpret it, isn't.

25
00:01:27,279 --> 00:01:31,888
Regenerate just the seven flawed statics with that tighter prompt, and Harlan's

26
00:01:31,888 --> 00:01:36,321
batch goes from thirteen of twenty to twenty of twenty, without touching the

27
00:01:36,321 --> 00:01:38,188
thirteen that were already fine.

28
00:01:38,188 --> 00:01:43,088
Keep the reference image itself somewhere permanent, not just pasted into one prompt

29
00:01:43,088 --> 00:01:43,905
and forgotten.

30
00:01:43,905 --> 00:01:48,805
The next batch, and the one after that, should start from the same locked reference,

31
00:01:48,805 --> 00:01:51,372
not a fresh description written from memory.

32
00:01:51,372 --> 00:01:56,214
That's how "Harlan Navy" stays one specific colour across months of batches instead

33
00:01:56,214 --> 00:01:59,422
of drifting a little every time someone redescribes it.

34
00:01:59,422 --> 00:02:03,856
Notice what this fix cost: one afternoon, a saved reference image, and seven

35
00:02:03,856 --> 00:02:04,673
regenerations.

36
00:02:04,673 --> 00:02:09,456
Compare that to a full reshoot catching the same problem, which would mean another

37
00:02:09,456 --> 00:02:11,906
studio booking to fix a detail this small.

38
00:02:11,906 --> 00:02:16,748
That gap is most of the argument for this entire section, made concrete on one real

39
00:02:16,748 --> 00:02:19,607
mistake instead of stated as a general principle.

40
00:02:20,157 --> 00:02:25,029
Colour is the easiest brand-control check, which is exactly why it's tempting to

41
00:02:25,029 --> 00:02:25,699
stop there.

42
00:02:25,699 --> 00:02:27,831
It isn't the only one that matters.

43
00:02:27,831 --> 00:02:32,703
A generator is optimising for a pleasing image, not for matching Harlan's actual

44
00:02:32,703 --> 00:02:33,190
product.

45
00:02:33,190 --> 00:02:38,124
It can quietly round off the exact cut, smooth a texture, or invent a detail that

46
00:02:38,124 --> 00:02:42,935
wasn't in the reference at all, and none of that shows up if the only thing you

47
00:02:42,935 --> 00:02:44,458
checked was the hex code.

48
00:02:44,458 --> 00:02:49,574
The real question, every time, is whether Dana would recognise the sock in the image

49
00:02:49,574 --> 00:02:51,401
as the one she actually sells.

50
00:02:51,401 --> 00:02:56,213
This check takes real product knowledge, which is exactly why it can't be fully

51
00:02:56,213 --> 00:02:58,223
delegated to a generic checklist.

52
00:02:58,223 --> 00:03:03,095
Whoever runs it needs to have actually held the product, the same way Dana would

53
00:03:03,095 --> 00:03:08,150
notice a subtly wrong cuff in half a second that a checklist item would never think

54
00:03:08,150 --> 00:03:08,942
to ask about.

55
00:03:08,942 --> 00:03:13,144
That's also why this check doesn't scale the way the colour fix does.

56
00:03:13,144 --> 00:03:17,530
A hex value is a fact anyone on the team can verify against a reference.

57
00:03:17,530 --> 00:03:22,646
Product accuracy needs someone who's actually handled the sock, which means it stays

58
00:03:22,646 --> 00:03:27,153
a human review step even once everything else in this workflow is routine.

59
00:03:27,703 --> 00:03:32,587
The moment a lifestyle static includes a face, the risk profile changes completely,

60
00:03:32,587 --> 00:03:34,117
and it's worth naming why.

61
00:03:34,117 --> 00:03:38,824
Humans are unusually good at detecting a face that's almost right but not quite:

62
00:03:38,824 --> 00:03:43,473
proportions a little off, an expression that doesn't track the scene, skin that

63
00:03:43,473 --> 00:03:45,297
reads as too smooth to be real.

64
00:03:45,297 --> 00:03:49,946
That's the uncanny valley, and it doesn't need to be dramatically wrong to work

65
00:03:49,946 --> 00:03:50,652
against you.

66
00:03:50,652 --> 00:03:55,419
A face that's ninety-five percent right often reads worse than one that's plainly

67
00:03:55,419 --> 00:04:00,362
stylised, because ninety-five percent right is exactly what triggers the discomfort.

68
00:04:00,362 --> 00:04:05,187
This is a genuinely different failure mode from the colour drift a few slides ago.

69
00:04:05,187 --> 00:04:08,836
A wrong hex value is a fact you can check against a reference.

70
00:04:08,836 --> 00:04:13,779
An uncanny face is a feeling most viewers can't name, which makes it harder to catch

71
00:04:13,779 --> 00:04:18,427
on a checklist and easier to miss until real spend has already gone against it.

72
00:04:18,977 --> 00:04:22,322
Here's the honest call this section doesn't want to avoid.

73
00:04:22,322 --> 00:04:27,052
If a face isn't earning its place in an angle, the safest move is to leave it out.

74
00:04:27,052 --> 00:04:31,781
A clean product shot with nobody in frame essentially never triggers this problem.

75
00:04:31,781 --> 00:04:36,452
A face that's almost right, sitting in a batch next to nineteen other statics, is

76
00:04:36,452 --> 00:04:39,797
the one thing a viewer will remember for the wrong reason.

77
00:04:39,797 --> 00:04:44,642
You'll meet a more deliberate answer to faces in Section four, where video needs one

78
00:04:44,642 --> 00:04:49,025
on purpose, handled properly rather than as a side effect of a product shot.

79
00:04:49,025 --> 00:04:53,812
Ask yourself, honestly, whether the angle actually needs a person in it, or whether

80
00:04:53,812 --> 00:04:56,292
a person just felt like the default choice.

81
00:04:56,292 --> 00:05:01,078
"The 15-20 spec" and "not just for flights" from the angle map don't need a face at

82
00:05:01,078 --> 00:05:01,309
all.

83
00:05:01,309 --> 00:05:03,616
"A nurse's honest review" probably does.

84
00:05:03,616 --> 00:05:06,673
Not every row on that map should get the same answer.

85
00:05:07,223 --> 00:05:10,954
Do you actually need a person for what this angle is arguing?

86
00:05:10,954 --> 00:05:15,848
Run that question down your own angle map before you generate a single face, and

87
00:05:15,848 --> 00:05:20,069
you'll usually find the honest answer is no more often than it's yes.

88
00:05:20,069 --> 00:05:25,146
Two of Harlan's three angles here don't need a face at all, and that ratio holds up

89
00:05:25,146 --> 00:05:30,039
on most angle maps once you actually ask the question instead of defaulting to a

90
00:05:30,039 --> 00:05:34,321
lifestyle shot because that's what a shoot would have produced anyway.

91
00:05:34,321 --> 00:05:36,585
A generator makes adding a face free.

92
00:05:36,585 --> 00:05:39,643
It doesn't make adding one a good idea by default.

93
00:05:40,193 --> 00:05:45,062
Next lecture closes the second half of Harlan's first-batch problem: the mmHg

94
00:05:45,062 --> 00:05:49,804
overlay that garbled on five of twenty, and the workflow that keeps it from

95
00:05:49,804 --> 00:05:50,816
happening again.

96
00:05:50,816 --> 00:05:55,495
Before that, do the five-minute version of what just fixed Harlan's batch.

97
00:05:55,495 --> 00:06:00,237
Pull your own product's exact colour as a hex code, not a word anyone could

98
00:06:00,237 --> 00:06:05,486
interpret differently, and keep one clean reference image somewhere you'll actually

99
00:06:05,486 --> 00:06:06,371
find it again.

100
00:06:06,371 --> 00:06:11,366
That pair, the hex value and the reference image, is the lock Section 3.6's B03

101
00:06:11,366 --> 00:06:13,516
workshop assumes you already have.

102
00:06:13,516 --> 00:06:17,499
Without it, your own batch drifts exactly the way Harlan's did.
